A||N||9||Bob Englert||87 GT tach jumped, car died||||1||
Z||000000||Bob Englert||03-07-2001||10:20 PM||||I was just starting a business trip in my 87 GT when I noticed the tach was jumping wildly and it felt like it was starting to stumble and miss.<P>I turned it around and started to limp it home and it died.  After about 1 minute it started and a was able to limp it home, it ran OK until I revved it past 3K or so, when it started jumping the tach and it died again.  <P>The problem is obviously ignition related, and I plan to do a full tune-up.  I was wondering if anyone has any suggestions on what I should be looking for for this problem.  The car has 95K.  I plan to replace the ignition module coil.  Could the tach filter cause the problems I am seeing?<P>Any ideas before I dig into this would be appreciated.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000001||RNGCRSH||03-07-2001||10:54 PM||||check the tach filter.||||reg||1
Z||000002||loafer87gt||03-07-2001||11:44 PM||||Had the same problem last summer, was quite a nightmare trying to track down the culprit. In the end, it turned out it was a $20 tach filter, which thanks to the forum, I found and replaced myself. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001019-2-003266.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001019-2-003266.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||03-07-2001||11:53 PM||||A tach filter can cause a problem bigger than a jumpy tach.. Why the heck is it there anyways? Leave it up to GM to NOT put it in the actual tachometer.||||reg||1
Z||000004||pHoOl||03-08-2001||12:28 AM||||I had a problem that was similar with my 88, but it ended up being some corroded parts in the distributor.<P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who can't outrun the cops.<P>AIM: phool88fiero<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.gvsuphool.homestead.com/files/pHoOl_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||G-Nasty||03-08-2001||12:38 AM||||Thats happened twice to me. It turned out one of my plug wires was history. The car will stutter and the tach will jump around.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||03-08-2001||09:30 AM||||Mine started doing this intermittantly. I  replaced the ignition module and coil as a precaution. (Didn't want to get stranded. Atlanta expressways are insane.) Still running on the original tach filter. No more problems. Most folks recommend that the module be a GM Delco part, and be sure to use the heat-sink compound.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||03-08-2001||01:29 PM||||the di-electric grease is a must or you'll weld the part to the distributor. the tach filter can be taken off and thrown. just wire through it. one white wire 5 min with a soldering iron and POOF no more tach problems. as for the dieing.. the lead into and out of the dist may be corroded.. use the same di-electric grease supplied in the ICM to keep the connections free of corosion.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Bob Englert||03-08-2001||01:45 PM||||Thanks all for the info.<P>Since I have only had the car for about 5 months, I think I will do a complete tuneup and replace the distributor, coil, module, cap, rotor, wires, and plugs.  I feel better knowing that I have "baselined" almost all wear items in the ignition system.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000009||Bob Englert||03-09-2001||11:35 PM||||JUst did my major tuneup and the car is running great again.  Now I can take it to our Fiero meeting in Hickory!<P>I had wanted to replace the ignition components anyway, and I did see sparks around the heat shields in the evening, so a good tuneup was due.<P>The tach filter is the only thing I did not replace.  I will probably order one from Rodney so it will be on hand if needed.<P>Thanks for all the input.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
